  "body": "## Summary\n`PrecompileReader.suppliedBalance()` reads HyperCore PM supplied balance state from `PRECOMPILE_SUPPLIED_BALANCE`, but it returns only `supply.value` and discards `borrow.value`. When backing is calculated, the corresponding borrow liability is not deducted. Under Portfolio Margin, delayed CoreWriter order execution can create borrow against eligible collateral, so `totalBacking()` and `surplus()` can be overstated and yield can be settled against assets that are not net backing.\n\n## Finding description\nIn `suppliedBalance()`, the contract calls `PRECOMPILE_SUPPLIED_BALANCE` to read the account's PM supplied balance:\n\n[PrecompileReader.sol#L85-L91](https://github.com/code-423n4/2026-04-monetrix/blob/3d94be1361ca01d959f9165a78f0d75c5657fe3e/src/core/PrecompileReader.sol#L85-L91)\n```solidity\n    function suppliedBalance(address account, uint64 token) internal view returns (uint64 supplied) {\n        (bool ok, bytes memory res) = HyperCoreConstants.PRECOMPILE_SUPPLIED_BALANCE.staticcall(\n            abi.encode(account, token)\n        );\n        require(ok && res.length >= 128, \"PrecompileReader: supplied balance read failed\");\n@>      (,,, supplied) = abi.decode(res, (uint64, uint64, uint64, uint64));\n    }\n```\nAccording to the `L1Read.sol` example linked from Hyperliquid's [HyperCore interaction docs](https://hyperliquid.gitbook.io/hyperliquid-docs/for-developers/hyperevm/interacting-with-hypercore), this state contains both borrow and supply fields:\n```solidity\n  struct BasisAndValue {\n    uint64 basis;\n    uint64 value;\n  }\n\n  struct BorrowLendUserTokenState {\n    BasisAndValue borrow;\n    BasisAndValue supply;\n  }\n```\n\nTherefore, the decoded tuple corresponds to `borrow.basis`, `borrow.value`, `supply.basis`, and `supply.value`. The current implementation assigns only the fourth value to `supplied`, ignores the second value, and returns a gross supplied amount instead of signed net borrow/lend state.\n\nUnder Hyperliquid [Portfolio Margin](https://hyperliquid.gitbook.io/hyperliquid-docs/trading/portfolio-margin), an account placing spot or perp orders with insufficient idle balance can automatically borrow against eligible collateral:\n\n> insufficient balance will automatically borrowed against eligible collateral ...\n\nBecause this borrow must be repaid and accrues interest continuously, it is a liability that must reduce net backing.\n\nThis can occur even when the EVM side order parameters appear funded, because CoreWriter order actions are not executed immediately after they are enqueued. Hyperliquid's [docs](https://hyperliquid.gitbook.io/hyperliquid-docs/for-developers/hyperevm/interacting-with-hypercore) state that order actions and vault transfers sent through CoreWriter are delayed onchain for a few seconds. During this delay, the actual HyperCore execution state can differ from the EVM side estimate. For example:\n- USDC can be reserved by other pending or open orders.\n- The spot order can fill while the perp order is delayed or partially filled.\n- Price changes can make the spot purchase require more USDC than estimated on EVM.\n- The vault L1 account can have eligible PM collateral but insufficient idle USDC.\n- Other CoreWriter actions can change balances or margin state during the delay.\n\nOne concrete path is `executeHedge()`: a hedge that looked fully funded when the orders were enqueued can later execute with a PM borrow on HyperCore. That borrow must reduce backing, but Monetrix does not read it.\n\nWhen backing is calculated, `suppliedUsdcEvm()` and `suppliedNotionalUsdcFromPerp()` both rely on `suppliedBalance()`:\n\n[MonetrixAccountant.sol#L138-L172](https://github.com/code-423n4/2026-04-monetrix/blob/3d94be1361ca01d959f9165a78f0d75c5657fe3e/src/core/MonetrixAccountant.sol#L138-L172)\n```solidity\n   function _readL1Backing(address account, SuppliedAsset[] storage suppliedList)\n        internal view returns (int256 total)\n    {\n        ...\n        // Supplied (0x811) — iterate only registered slots; strict reads.\n        uint256 slen = suppliedList.length;\n        for (uint256 i = 0; i < slen; i++) {\n            SuppliedAsset storage a = suppliedList[i];\n            if (a.spotToken == uint64(HyperCoreConstants.USDC_TOKEN_INDEX)) {\n@>              total += int256(PrecompileReader.suppliedUsdcEvm(account));\n            } else {\n                total += int256(\n@>                  PrecompileReader.suppliedNotionalUsdcFromPerp(uint32(a.spotToken), a.perpIndex, account)\n                );\n            }\n        }\n        ...\n    }\n```\n\nBecause only the supply side is added and the borrow side is not deducted, PM borrow can make reported backing higher than the account's net assets. The issue is especially visible when the borrow is on USDC, because `_readL1Backing()` only iterates registered supplied slots and does not independently read USDC PM borrow unless USDC itself is registered.\n\n## Impact\nSettlement yield can be calculated from overstated `totalBackingSigned()` and `surplus()` when PM automatically creates a borrow, allowing the protocol to distribute yield that is not backed by net assets.\n\nThis causes direct loss to protocol backing and can leave insufficient assets to cover future redemptions.\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\nUpdate the backing calculation to include PM borrow liabilities through the following steps:\n1. Decode both `borrow.value` and `supply.value` from `PRECOMPILE_SUPPLIED_BALANCE`.\n2. Account for PM balances as signed net values, where `net = supply.value - borrow.value`, and add this signed net value to `MonetrixAccountant.totalBackingSigned()` instead of adding only gross supplied balances. The net value must remain negative when `borrow.value > supply.value`.\n3. Also query the USDC token's PM state and deduct `borrow.value` for USDC borrow created by PM spot funding. Do not rely only on the registered spot token supplied list, because the borrow can appear on the USDC token response.",
  "proof_of_concept": "First, add the following imports in `test/c4/C4Submission.t.sol`:\n```solidity\nimport {ActionEncoder} from \"../../src/core/ActionEncoder.sol\";\nimport {PrecompileReader} from \"../../src/core/PrecompileReader.sol\";\n```\n\nThen replace `test_submissionValidity()` in `test/c4/C4Submission.t.sol`:\n```solidity\n    // Attack Path:\n    // 1. user1 deposits 1,000 USDC, the vault mints 1,000 USDM, and real backing is 1,000 USDC.\n    // 2. Governance whitelists perpIndex = 4, spotToken = 1105, spotPairAssetId = 11105, then enables PM.\n    // 3. The operator enqueues a 100 USDC notional hedge, and PM mode registers spotToken as a supplied slot.\n    // 4. At delayed HyperCore execution, the vault has eligible PM collateral but insufficient idle USDC,\n    //    so HyperCore can automatically create a 10 USDC borrow under PM.\n    // 5. Metadata and oracle mocks value the supplied spotToken at 100 USDC.\n    // 6. The USDC supplied-balance response proves borrow.value = 10 USDC and supply.value = 0.\n    // 7. totalBacking() incorrectly reports 1,100 USDC, and surplus() incorrectly reports 100 USDC.\n    // 8. Correct backing must subtract borrow.value, leaving 1,090 USDC, so ignored borrow overstates\n    //    backing by 10 USDC.\n    function test_submissionValidity() public {\n        // Step 1 - Prepare the 1,000 USDC deposit and the later 100 USDC supplied\n        // spot collateral plus 10 USDC borrow amounts. The deposit below mints\n        // 1,000 USDM, and real backing starts at 1,000 USDC.\n        uint256 initialDeposit = 1_000e6;\n        uint32 perpIndex = 4;\n        uint32 spotToken = 1105;\n        uint32 spotPairAssetId = 11105;\n\n        uint64 suppliedSpotWei = 100e8;\n        uint64 borrowedUsdcL1 = uint64(10e6 * 100);\n\n        _deposit(user1, initialDeposit);\n\n        // Step 2 - Governance whitelists the hedge pair and enables PM,\n        // making later supplied-balance reads relevant to accounting.\n        vm.startPrank(admin);\n        config.addTradeableAsset(\n            MonetrixConfig.TradeableAsset({\n                perpIndex: perpIndex,\n                spotIndex: spotToken,\n                spotPairAssetId: spotPairAssetId\n            })\n        );\n        vault.setPmEnabled(true);\n        vm.stopPrank();\n\n        // Step 3 - Build a 100 USDC notional hedge that will be enqueued\n        // through CoreWriter. PM mode registers spotToken in vaultSupplied\n        // when executeHedge runs, and the assertions prove that registration.\n        ActionEncoder.HedgeParams memory params = ActionEncoder.HedgeParams({\n            spotAsset: spotPairAssetId,\n            perpAsset: perpIndex,\n            size: suppliedSpotWei,\n            spotPrice: uint64(1e8),\n            perpPrice: uint64(1e8),\n            cloid: 0,\n            tif: uint8(3),\n            spotReduceOnly: false,\n            perpReduceOnly: false\n        });\n\n        vm.prank(operator);\n        vault.executeHedge(6, params);\n\n        assertEq(accountant.vaultSuppliedLength(), 1);\n        (uint64 registeredSpotToken, uint32 registeredPerp) = accountant.vaultSupplied(0);\n        assertEq(registeredSpotToken, uint64(spotToken));\n        assertEq(registeredPerp, perpIndex);\n\n        // Step 4 - The vault has eligible PM collateral, but only 90 USDC of idle\n        // USDC is available for the 100 USDC spot buy at delayed HyperCore execution.\n        // Other pending CoreWriter actions or L1 state changes can consume idle USDC\n        // before execution. PM can automatically borrow the missing 10 USDC.\n        _PoCMockPrecompile(payable(HyperCoreConstants.PRECOMPILE_SUPPLIED_BALANCE)).setResponse(\n            abi.encode(address(vault), uint64(HyperCoreConstants.USDC_TOKEN_INDEX)),\n            abi.encode(uint64(0), borrowedUsdcL1, uint64(0), uint64(0))\n        );\n\n        // Step 4 - The spot token remains supplied with 100 USDC notional value\n        // while the USDC borrow is represented in the separate USDC token response.\n        _PoCMockPrecompile(payable(HyperCoreConstants.PRECOMPILE_SUPPLIED_BALANCE)).setResponse(\n            abi.encode(address(vault), uint64(spotToken)),\n            abi.encode(uint64(0), uint64(0), uint64(0), suppliedSpotWei)\n        );\n\n        // Step 5 - Metadata and oracle mocks let the accountant value\n        // spotToken = 1105 at 1 USDC.\n        uint64[] memory emptySpots = new uint64[](0);\n        PrecompileReader.TokenInfo memory tokenInfo = PrecompileReader.TokenInfo({\n            name: \"HYPE\",\n            spots: emptySpots,\n            deployerTradingFeeShare: 0,\n            deployer: address(0),\n            evmContract: address(0),\n            szDecimals: 6,\n            weiDecimals: 8,\n            evmExtraWeiDecimals: 0\n        });\n        PrecompileReader.PerpAssetInfo memory perpInfo = PrecompileReader.PerpAssetInfo({\n            coin: \"HYPE\",\n            marginTableId: 0,\n            szDecimals: 6,\n            maxLeverage: 50,\n            onlyIsolated: false\n        });\n        _PoCMockPrecompile(payable(HyperCoreConstants.PRECOMPILE_TOKEN_INFO)).setResponse(\n            abi.encode(spotToken),\n            abi.encode(tokenInfo)\n        );\n        _PoCMockPrecompile(payable(HyperCoreConstants.PRECOMPILE_PERP_ASSET_INFO)).setResponse(\n            abi.encode(perpIndex),\n            abi.encode(perpInfo)\n        );\n        _PoCMockPrecompile(payable(HyperCoreConstants.PRECOMPILE_ORACLE_PX)).setResponse(\n            abi.encode(perpIndex),\n            abi.encode(uint64(1))\n        );\n\n        // Step 6 - The raw USDC supplied-balance response proves the mocked\n        // HyperCore state includes borrow.value.\n        (bool ok, bytes memory rawBorrowState) = HyperCoreConstants.PRECOMPILE_SUPPLIED_BALANCE.staticcall(\n            abi.encode(address(vault), uint64(HyperCoreConstants.USDC_TOKEN_INDEX))\n        );\n        assertTrue(ok);\n\n        (, uint64 borrowValue,, uint64 supplyValue) = abi.decode(rawBorrowState, (uint64, uint64, uint64, uint64));\n        assertEq(borrowValue, borrowedUsdcL1);\n        assertEq(supplyValue, 0);\n\n        // Step 7 - Monetrix counts the supplied collateral but ignores the\n        // 10 USDC borrow, so reported backing is overstated by 10 USDC.\n        uint256 reportedBacking = accountant.totalBacking();\n\n        assertEq(reportedBacking, 1_100e6, \"gross supplied collateral is counted\");\n        assertEq(accountant.surplus(), int256(100e6), \"ignored borrow creates phantom surplus\");\n\n        // Step 8 - Convert the 10 USDC borrow to EVM units and subtract it.\n        // The correct backing is 1,090 USDC, not the reported 1,100 USDC.\n        uint256 correctBacking = reportedBacking - (uint256(borrowValue) / 100);\n        assertEq(correctBacking, 1_090e6, \"correct backing must subtract borrow.value\");\n        assertEq(reportedBacking - correctBacking, 10e6, \"ignored borrow overstates backing by 10 USDC\");\n    }\n```\n\nRun:\n```shell\nforge test --match-path \"test/c4/C4Submission.t.sol\" -vvv\n```",
